+## Private Cache and Eviction
 
 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### Eviction Policy: 2Q
+
+The cache uses a **two-queue (2Q) eviction policy** to protect comparison sheets during long sequential screenings.
+
+The cache is divided into two regions:
+
+- **Queue A1** (new/in): contains entries on their first admission. Occupies at most 1/3 of the cache capacity (4 GiB).
+- **Queue A2** (reused): contains entries that have been accessed at least twice (once on their initial admission, then reaccessed). Occupies the remaining 2/3 capacity (8 GiB).
+
+When a preview is admitted to the cache:
+- New previews enter Queue A1
+- If a preview is already in the cache and is accessed again, it is promoted from A1 to A2
+- If promoting to A2 would exceed the A2 limit, evict entries from A2 (least recently used first)
+- When A1 exceeds its limit, evict entries from A1 (in arrival order, FIFO)
+
+This policy resists eviction of frequently reused comparison sheets during large sequential screenings. One-off preview requests fill A1 without displacing clips that editors repeatedly visit.
+
+The queue sizes and eviction order are private to the preview worker and not exposed through any external interface.
